%I A398929 #42 Aug 27 2026 11:02:30
%S A398929 2,2,5,5,7,8,10,10,13,13,15,16,18,18,21,21,23,24,26,26,29,29,31,32,34,
%T A398929 34,37,37,39,40,42,42,45,45,47,48,50,50,53,53,55,56,58,58,61,61,63,64,
%U A398929 66,66,69,69,71,72,74,74,77,77,79,80,82,82,85,85,87,88,90,90,93,93,95
%N A398929 Number of sets R(k) that contain n, where R(k) is the set of k consecutive integers centered at k (excluding k if k is even, including k if k is odd).
%C A398929 For each k >= 1, R(k) is the set of k integers closest to k, with the rule that k is omitted if k is even and included if k is odd. The sum of the elements of R(k) is k^2. a(n) is the number of such sets that contain n.
%H A398929 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A398929/b398929.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A398929 a(n) = n + floor(n/3) + (n mod 2).
%F A398929 Sum_{n>=1} (-1)^(n+1)/a(n) = log(1 + sqrt(2))/(4*sqrt(2)) - (sqrt(2)+1)*Pi/16 + log(2)/2. - _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 27 2026
%e A398929 a(1)=2 because 1 appears in R(1)={1} and R(2)={1,3}.
%e A398929 a(3)=5 because 3 appears in R(2), R(3), R(4), R(5), R(6).
%t A398929 A398929[n_] := n + Quotient[n, 3] + Mod[n, 2]; Array[A398929, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Aug 25 2026 *)
%o A398929 (Python)
%o A398929 def a(n): return n + n//3 + (n%2)
%o A398929 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 101)])
%o A398929 (PARI) a(n) = n + n \ 3 + n % 2; \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 27 2026
